.Dd $Mdocdate$
.Dt FVWM_EXEC 1
.Os OpenBSD
.Sh NAME
.Nm fvwm_exec
.Nd privilege-separated execution helper for
.Xr fvwm 1
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Nm
is the execution helper of the
.Xr fvwm 1
window manager.
It is started by
.Xr fvwm 1
at session startup, before the window manager locks its filesystem
view, and communicates with it over an
.Xr imsg 3
channel (a
.Xr socketpair 2
whose descriptor is passed in the
.Ev FVWM_EXEC_FD
environment variable).
.Pp
.Nm
handles two request classes:
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Ic Exec
Launch a program detached from
.Xr fvwm 1 .
.It Ic PipeRead
Run a command with its standard output streamed back to
.Xr fvwm 1 ,
which feeds it to its configuration parser.
.El
.Pp
Every request payload is validated against the message length;
oversized or malformed requests are discarded.
A PipeRead command whose output cannot be consumed promptly is
terminated with
.Dv SIGTERM
followed by
.Dv SIGKILL
after a grace period.
.Pp
Because the helper runs commands that may need the user's full
privileges, it deliberately applies no
.Xr pledge 2
or
.Xr unveil 2
policy: both are inherited across
.Xr execve 2
and would cripple the launched programs.
Its protection comes from its small dedicated code base, strict
message validation, and
.Xr closefrom 3
descriptor hygiene; it never inherits the X11 connection.
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr fvwm 1 ,
.Xr execve 2 ,
.Xr closefrom 3 ,
.Xr imsg_init 3 ,
.Xr pledge 2 ,
.Xr unveil 2
.Sh HISTORY
.Nm
first appeared as part of the OpenBSD fork of FVWM 2.2.5.
.Sh CAVEATS
.Nm
should only be started by
.Xr fvwm 1 :
it expects the imsg descriptor in
.Ev FVWM_EXEC_FD
and takes no arguments.